body:
## Problem

generateMarkdown terminates the process with log.Fatalf on two recoverable conditions — an unreadable template file and a template with the wrong separator count — instead of returning an error to its caller. It is a pure rendering helper that has been given the authority to kill the program.

## Evidence

main.go:148-159

    templateData, err := os.ReadFile(templateFile)
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatalf("Error reading template file: %v", err)
    }

    templateParts := strings.SplitN(string(templateData), "---", 3)
    if len(templateParts) != 3 {
        log.Fatalf("Invalid template format")
    }

It is reached at main.go:107, after every feed in the config has already been fetched over the network.

## Impact

A one-character typo in template.md wastes the entire fetch cycle and produces no output. Worse, the failure is only discovered after all network work is done, so the cost of a trivial config mistake is a full run. log.Fatalf also calls os.Exit, which skips deferred functions, so this pattern is a live hazard for any future cleanup added to main. "Invalid template format" additionally names neither the file nor what was expected, leaving the operator to guess.

## Fix

Return the error and let main decide:

    func generateMarkdown(templateFile string, articlesByWeek map[string][]Article, weeks []string) (string, error) {
        templateData, err := os.ReadFile(templateFile)
        if err != nil {
            return "", fmt.Errorf("reading template %s: %w", templateFile, err)
        }
        parts := strings.SplitN(string(templateData), "---", 3)
        if len(parts) != 3 {
            return "", fmt.Errorf("template %s: want a header and footer separated by two --- lines, found %d section(s)", templateFile, len(parts))
        }
        ...
    }

Then read and validate the template at the top of main, before any feed is fetched, so a bad template fails in under a second instead of after the full fetch.

## Resolution
Template reading and splitting moved out of generateMarkdown into loadTemplate, which returns (header, footer, error) with a message naming the file and the section count found. main calls it before the fetch loop, so a malformed template now fails in under a second instead of after every feed has been fetched. generateMarkdown is now pure and takes the header and footer as strings; no log.Fatalf remains outside main.

## Problem

All four regexps in getURLDomain are built with regexp.MustCompile inside the function body, so every call re-parses and re-compiles them. The function runs once per feed item. Three of the four patterns are also doing work that plain string operations cover.

## Evidence

main.go:199-208

    if regexp.MustCompile(`^https?`).MatchString(urlString) {
    ...
    if regexp.MustCompile(`^www\.`).MatchString(urlString) {
        urlString = regexp.MustCompile(`^www\.`).ReplaceAllString(urlString, "")
    }

    return regexp.MustCompile(`([a-z0-9\-]+\.)+[a-z0-9\-]+`).FindString(urlString)

Note `^www\.` is compiled twice in the same branch. Called from fetchArticles at main.go:134, once per item across all feeds.

## Impact

Small in absolute terms for a weekly cron job over a few hundred items, so this is a cleanliness issue rather than a hot path. It matters mainly because MustCompile panics on a bad pattern: keeping compilation inside a request-path function defers a failure that package-level vars would surface at process start. The two prefix checks are also more precisely expressed as string operations, which is the fix for the correctness defect filed separately as audit-2de2c1ba.

## Fix

Hoist the one regexp that genuinely needs to be one, and replace the prefix patterns with strings calls:

    var domainRe = regexp.MustCompile(`([a-z0-9\-]+\.)+[a-z0-9\-]+`)

    func getURLDomain(urlString string) string {
        urlString = strings.TrimSpace(urlString)
        if strings.HasPrefix(urlString, "http://") || strings.HasPrefix(urlString, "https://") {
            if read, err := url.Parse(urlString); err == nil {
                urlString = read.Host
            }
        }
        urlString = strings.TrimPrefix(urlString, "www.")
        return domainRe.FindString(urlString)
    }

This is the same edit the Critical nil-deref fix needs, so apply them together.

## Resolution
The one regexp that needs to be one is now the package-level domainRe; the two prefix patterns became strings.HasPrefix and strings.TrimPrefix. The two Markdown escapers introduced for the injection fix are likewise package-level strings.Replacer values rather than per-article constructions. No MustCompile remains inside a per-item code path.
